<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Second Italo-Ethiopian War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1935">1935</a>–<a href="/years/1936">1936</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Italy's 1935 invasion · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Second Italo-Ethiopian War was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascist-italy">Fascist Italy</a>'s invasion and conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> in 1935–1936. Italian forces used aircraft and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poison-gas">poison gas</a>, and Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a> went into exile. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> imposed ineffective <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a>, and the conquest became a centrepiece of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-empire">Italian Empire</a> and a defining crisis for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-italo-ethiopian-war.jpg" alt="Second Italo-Ethiopian War" width="320" height="182" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Passo_Uarieu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> began to seek colonies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horn-of-africa">Horn of Africa</a> in the 1880s. That phase ended with the defeat of Italian forces at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa">Battle of Adwa</a> on 1 March 1896 by the army of Negus Menelik II. Afterwards Italy kept only Italian Eritrea and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Italian Somaliland</a>, and for decades its economic and diplomatic relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> stayed relatively stable.</p><p>In 1930 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> built a fort at the Walwal oasis in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogaden">Ogaden</a>, garrisoned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> dubats under Italian officers, well beyond the border set by the Italo-Ethiopian Treaty of 1928. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, preferring Italy as an ally against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, did little to discourage the Italian build-up. In April 1935 mobilisation began, and between February 1935 and January 1936 six army divisions were sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> and one to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 3 October 1935 Marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emilio-de-bono">Emilio De Bono</a> led some two hundred thousand soldiers across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mareb-river">Mareb River</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> without a declaration of war, while a smaller force under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rodolfo-graziani">Rodolfo Graziani</a> attacked from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>. Adwa fell on 6 October, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksum</a> on 15 October, where its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obelisk">obelisk</a> was taken down and sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Benito Mussolini, impatient at De Bono's caution, replaced him with General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pietro-badoglio">Pietro Badoglio</a> on 14 November.</p><p>An Ethiopian counterattack in December 1935 halted the advance for a time. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a> had authorised gas in an order of 27 October 1935, and the Regia Aeronautica dropped about 330 tons of mustard gas over the following months, against the 1925 Geneva Protocol. The Italians resumed the offensive in February 1936 and defeated the Ethiopian armies at Amba Aradam, Tembien and Shire, then won at Maychew on 31 March, ending organised resistance. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a> went into exile on 2 May, Badoglio entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a> on 5 May, and Mussolini announced the annexation on 9 May, forming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-east-africa">Italian East Africa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Hundreds of thousands of Ethiopian civilians died in the invasion, which some historians have described as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>. After an attempt on Graziani's life on 19 February 1937, Italian reprisals in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a> killed thousands; estimates range from a few hundred in Italian sources to 30,000 in Ethiopian ones. Resistance continued until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a> returned with British and Ethiopian forces in 1941 and entered Addis Ababa on 5 May.</p><p>The war was a propaganda success for the Fascist regime, but it strained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>'s budget. It ended the Stresa Front with the Western powers and led Italy towards Nazi Germany, while the failure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> to stop the invasion discredited that body in the years before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Second Italo-Ethiopian War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Italy's 1935 invasion</b>, which used aerial bombardment and poison gas to capture Addis Ababa in May 1936, was the gravest external threat since Ahmad Gran, but Haile Selassie returned in triumph on 5 May 1941 following British-assisted liberation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Second Italo-Ethiopian War</b> of 1935–1936, led by Emilio De Bono and then Pietro Badoglio, ended in the conquest of Ethiopia through numerical superiority, mechanised forces, aerial bombardment and the use of chemical weapons.</blockquote><a href="/empire/italian-empire">Italian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Haile Selassie made a pilgrimage to the churches during the <b>Second Italo-Ethiopian War</b>, at considerable risk of capture, before returning to his capital in April 1936.</blockquote><a href="/work/rock-hewn-churches-of-lalibela">Rock-hewn churches of Lalibela · Reception</a></li></ul></section>
